# MEMORY HANDOFF — SOCIAL MEDIA CONTENT AGENT BUILDS
## Context for whichever agent builds the next platform's system prompt (Instagram, TikTok, etc.)

You are being asked to build a subordinate-facing daily content prompt for a 
new social media platform, for the same underlying operator (Gaurav) who 
already has one working for Pinterest. This document is not the new prompt — 
it's what the Pinterest build actually taught, so you don't repeat the same 
mistakes or skip the same steps. Read this fully before doing anything else.

---

## THE GOAL, STATED PLAINLY

Gaurav runs a content operation. He has a subordinate (a real person, no prior 
platform/SEO/marketing knowledge) who executes a fixed daily workflow with zero 
strategic decisions. Every decision — what to post, where, in what format, 
linking to what — is made once, locked into the prompt, and never re-decided 
daily. The subordinate's only job is: open chat, get output, copy-paste, post.

The end target is a numeric outbound-traffic goal (Pinterest's was 500k 
clicks/month). Everything in the prompt should trace back to that number — if 
a design choice, a board, or a rule doesn't plausibly move that number, it's 
decoration and should be cut or deprioritized.

**You must become "a living algorithm of that platform."** That means before 
writing a single rule, you research deeply and current: how the platform's 
recommendation/discovery system actually works right now, what content formats 
are winning right now, what's changed recently, and what the platform's own 
stated best practices are. Do not build from pre-trained assumptions about how 
the platform "generally" works — platforms change their algorithms often, and 
stale assumptions produce a strategy that's already obsolete on day one.

---

## THE METHOD THAT ACTUALLY WORKED (follow this order)

1. **Understand the content supply first, not the platform second.** Before 
   any platform strategy was built, we audited the actual website content 
   (900+ URLs) to see what existed, how deep each topic cluster went, and 
   what was sustainable for months of daily output — not just what sounded 
   good. Board/theme selection was driven by content depth data, not vibes. 
   Do this same audit for whatever content pool feeds the new platform.

2. **Test the smallest real unit before scaling.** We did not write "the 
   Nano Banana prompt" as a whole system on paper and trust it. We generated 
   ONE test pin, looked at the actual rendered result, found a real bug (text 
   contrast failure — light text landed on a same-color background), and only 
   then generalized the fix into a rule. Every format (Direct Inspo, 
   Infographic, Informational) was proven on one real output before it became 
   a repeatable rule. If the new platform has an equivalent generative step 
   (image, video, caption), test it the same way — one real unit, inspect 
   the actual result, generalize only after it's proven.

3. **When something breaks, fix the root cause, not the instance.** The 
   contrast bug wasn't fixed by rewriting one prompt — it was fixed by adding 
   a standing rule ("text must never match the color of the surface behind 
   it") that now applies to every future generation, on every template, 
   forever. Look for the general rule behind every specific failure.

4. **Lock things in this order: content pool → format → volume/cadence → 
   platform-specific mechanics → wording.** We did NOT start with "what 
   should the caption say." We started with what content exists, then what 
   format best serves it, then how much/how often is sustainable and safe 
   per the platform's own norms, and only at the end did we touch exact 
   phrasing/templates. Wording is the last mile, not the starting point.

5. **Research safe volume/frequency limits from the platform itself before 
   fixing a daily number.** We did not guess "18 pins a day is fine." We 
   researched Pinterest's actual posting-frequency norms (3-7 pins/week/board 
   was the research finding) and only then negotiated a number with Gaurav 
   that balanced his desired volume against real platform risk (getting 
   flagged, suppressed, or diluted). Do the same research step for the new 
   platform — every platform has different safe-volume norms and different 
   penalties for over-posting.

