## MISTAKES I MADE — DO NOT REPEAT THESE

1. **I generated content from generic/imagined ideas instead of the actual 
   source material Gaurav provided.** Twice, I wrote a "test prompt" pulling 
   from general knowledge instead of the specific canonical link/page he'd 
   shared. He caught it both times and was right to be annoyed. **Rule: if 
   real source material exists, use it — never substitute invented content 
   because fetching the real thing is inconvenient.**

2. **I did basic arithmetic wrong out loud and had to be corrected.** Twice — 
   once on a pin-count total (12+6=24 instead of 18), once on a link-count 
   total (claimed 315 when the real number, after a silent data-loss bug in 
   my own file edit, was 256). **Rule: verify counts programmatically, not by 
   eyeballing or mental math, especially after any file edit — and always 
   re-check a total before stating it as fact.**

3. **I silently dropped real data during a file edit and reported a wrong 
   number confidently.** When inserting new content into the link-rotation 
   file, an entire 59-link block vanished and I didn't notice until asked to 
   verify. **Rule: after any structural edit to a data file (insertions, 
   renumbering, merges), run an explicit verification pass — check for gaps, 
   duplicates, and correct sequential order — before presenting it as done.**

4. **I over-explained prompts instead of matching the lean style the operator 
   actually wanted.** Gaurav explicitly modeled the tone he wanted (a 
   reference prompt written in short, tight, non-repetitive sentences) and I 
   initially kept writing padded, over-instructed prompts anyway. **Rule: 
   when the person shows you a reference example of the style they want, 
   match that style's density exactly — don't default to maximal explicitness 
   if they've signaled they want lean.**

5. **I built a structural decision (e.g. "match template to board mood") that 
   the operator explicitly didn't want, because I was solving a problem more 
   complex than the one asked.** He wanted true random template selection, 
   not curated matching. **Rule: don't add sophistication the person didn't 
   ask for — confirm scope before building a more elaborate mechanism than 
   requested.**

6. **I initially treated a "reference/inspiration" instruction as license to 
   loosely reinterpret a template, when the operator meant strict, pixel-level 
   reuse.** This produced a visibly worse result (a Halloween pin that changed 
   hand poses, badge shapes, and proportions instead of just the design). The 
   fix was reframing the instruction as an EDIT task ("modify this exact 
   file, do not create a new composition") rather than a "generate inspired 
   by this" task. **Rule: when an operator wants an existing asset reused 
   precisely, the generation instruction must say "edit this file, keep X/Y/Z 
   unchanged" — not "use this as a reference/inspiration," which gives the 
   generation model too much room to drift.**

7. **I misread pin-count instructions once (thought "3 boards daily" meant 
   3 pins, not 3 pins per board).** **Rule: when a volume/cadence instruction 
   is even slightly ambiguous, restate your interpretation back before 
   building on it, rather than assuming and having to redo work downstream.**

---

## WHAT ACTUALLY MATTERED MOST (in order of impact)

1. **A hardcoded weekly schedule so the subordinate never makes a judgment 
   call.** This was the single most important structural decision — every 
   board, every format, every day of the week is pre-decided. Nothing is 
   "ask the subordinate" or "let the model decide fresh each day" once locked.

2. **Deterministic, date-based rotation formulas for anything with too many 
   options to hardcode a fixed shortlist (e.g. 256 informational links).** 
   Rather than asking the model to "pick a good one" daily (which risks 
   drift, repetition, or mismatch), we used `(days since a fixed start date) 
   mod pool size` — fully reproducible, auditable, and requires zero judgment 
   from anyone, including the AI.

3. **A locked, tested visual/generation template library with explicit 
   "keep unchanged" and "change only" lists** — built from real reference 
   examples the operator provided, catalogued by structure (not theme, since 
   theme is disposable and gets overwritten daily).

4. **Truthfulness as a hard constraint on links** — every piece of content 
   must genuinely match where its outbound link goes. This wasn't just an 
   ethics nicety; it's what made the deterministic link-rotation mechanism 
   necessary in the first place (content gets built to match the day's 
   assigned link, not the other way around).

5. **A single, unambiguous output format the subordinate can mechanically 
   copy-paste** — every field (Title, Description, Prompt, Link, etc.) in 
   its own separate code block, label outside the block, nothing extra 
   inside it. This came from direct visual feedback (a screenshot of messy 
   output) — always sanity-check what the ACTUAL rendered output looks like 
   to a real, non-technical user, not just whether the instructions are 
   theoretically clear.

6. **A plain-language "how do I use this" walkthrough embedded directly in 
   the prompt**, written as numbered steps a first-time user follows exactly, 
   so the subordinate never has to guess at the workflow even before asking 
   a single question.

